@@ -0,0 +1,20 @@
+# OffscreenSoftwareFrame Object
+
+* `frameInfo` Object - The shared memory frame info.
+  * `widgetType` string - The widget type of the frame. Can be `popup` or `frame`.
+  * `pixelFormat` string - The pixel format of the frame.
+    * `rgba` - The frame format is 8-bit unorm RGBA.
+    * `bgra` - The frame format is 8-bit unorm BGRA.
+    * `rgbaf16` - The frame format is 16-bit float RGBA.
+  * `codedSize` [Size](size.md) - The full dimensions of the video frame.
+  * `contentRect` [Rectangle](rectangle.md) - The region of the video frame that capturer would like to populate. In OSR case, it is the same with `dirtyRect` that needs to be painted.
+  * `stride` number - The number of bytes between the starts of two consecutive rows in the shared memory.
+  * `dataSize` number - The number of bytes of pixel data in the shared memory region.
+  * `timestamp` number - The time in microseconds since the capture start.
+  * `metadata` Object - Extra metadata. See comments in src\media\base\video_frame_metadata.h for accurate details.
+    * `captureUpdateRect` [Rectangle](rectangle.md) (optional) - Updated area of frame, can be considered as the `dirty` area.
+    * `sourceSize` [Rectangle](rectangle.md) (optional) - Full size of the source frame.
+    * `frameCount` number (optional) - The increasing count of captured frame. May contain gaps if frames are dropped between two consecutively received frames.
+  * `fd` Integer _Linux_ - A file descriptor over the shared memory region holding the pixels, valid until `release()` is called.
+* `release` Function - Release the resources. The `softwareFrame` cannot be directly passed to another process, users need to maintain frame lifecycles in
+  main process. Only a limited number of frames can exist at the same time, so it's important that you call `softwareFrame.release()` as soon as you're done with the frame.
